		id: 'what-grace-means',
		heading: 'What grace means',
		bodyHtml: `<p><em>Grace</em> means <strong>a gift you did not earn, given because of the giver&rsquo;s love</strong>. In the Bible it is our Father&rsquo;s favour toward people who could never repay Him, and His power at work in them. It is not a reward for good behaviour and not a loophole for bad behaviour. It is the Father giving Himself.</p><p>Paul puts it at the centre of the gospel: &ldquo;For by grace you have been saved through faith&mdash;not your own doing; it is the gift of our Father. Not from works, so that no one may boast&rdquo; (Ephesians 2:8&ndash;9). Grace is the &ldquo;gift&rdquo; in that sentence, and the giver is named.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'hebrew-and-greek',
		heading: 'The Hebrew and Greek words behind “grace”',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The New Testament word is the Greek <em>charis</em>, the ordinary word for a favour freely given or a gift that delights. Our word <em>charity</em> and the Greek word for &ldquo;gift,&rdquo; <em>charisma</em>, come from the same root.</p><p>In the Old Testament two Hebrew words carry the idea. <em>Chen</em> is favour, as when someone &ldquo;finds favour&rdquo; in another&rsquo;s eyes. And <em>hesed</em> is the great covenant word: loyal, steadfast, never-letting-go love. Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible&trade; renders <em>hesed</em> as &ldquo;covenant love.&rdquo; When our Father describes Himself to Moses He uses both ideas at once: &ldquo;your Father&mdash;compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, and overflowing with covenant love and faithfulness&rdquo; (Exodus 34:6).</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'grace-in-jesus',
		heading: 'Grace came in a person',
