	},
	{
		id: 'grace-for-today',
		heading: 'Grace for weakness, every day',
		bodyHtml: `<p>Grace is not only how the story begins. When Paul begged to be rid of a weakness, the answer was, &ldquo;My grace is enough for you, for my power is perfected in weakness&rdquo; (2 Corinthians 12:9). Grace meets us where we are not strong.</p><p>It also changes how we live. &ldquo;For the grace of our Father has appeared, bringing salvation to all people, teaching us to reject wickedness and worldly desires&rdquo; (Titus 2:11&ndash;12). That is why the letter to the Hebrews can say, &ldquo;Let us then approach the throne of grace boldly, so we may receive mercy and find grace to help us in time of need&rdquo; (Hebrews 4:16). Children do not approach a Father&rsquo;s throne afraid.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'grace-and-mercy',
		heading: 'Grace and mercy: the difference',
		bodyHtml: `<p>A simple way to hold the two together: <strong>mercy</strong> is not receiving the punishment we deserve; <strong>grace</strong> is receiving the gift we do not deserve. Hebrews 4:16 names both in one breath, &ldquo;receive mercy and find grace.&rdquo; Our Father does both, because He is &ldquo;compassionate and gracious, slow to anger and abounding in covenant love&rdquo; (Psalm 103:8).</p>`,
	},
];

